\details{
For one profile, write \eqn{S(A, t \mid z)} for the probability that the
profile occupies a state in the state set \eqn{A} at visit \eqn{t} when the
compared variable is set to \eqn{z}. With reference value \eqn{z_0} and
comparison value \eqn{z_1}, \code{estimand = "sop"} returns
\eqn{S(A, t \mid z_1) - S(A, t \mid z_0)}, or their ratio. Time in state
adds these probabilities over the requested visits; with \code{time_map}, it
integrates them over real time from \code{baseline_time}, starting from the
profile's starting state, or from the state set in \code{variables} when it sets
the previous state. Time benefit compares the profile's two state
distributions at each visit, as described in \code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}}.

For differences and time benefit, the mean of these comparisons over the
profiles equals the \code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}} estimate. For ratios it does not,
because \code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}} divides averaged probabilities.

For frequentist models, pass the result to \code{\link[=inferences]{inferences()}}. Because each
profile's starting state and covariates are held as given, every method
reflects uncertainty in the model coefficients only:
\itemize{
\item \code{method = "delta"} uses conditional variance. For one profile, the
derivative of a difference with respect to the coefficients is the
derivative of the comparison scenario's summary minus that of the
reference scenario, and its variance is \eqn{G V_\beta G^\top}, where
\eqn{G} holds these derivatives and \eqn{V_\beta} is the coefficient
covariance. It supports differences of SOPs and of time in states for
first-order full proportional-odds \code{orm}, \code{vglm}, and \code{robcov_vglm}
fits; \code{\link[stats:vcov]{stats::vcov()}} then returns the covariance between rows.
\item \code{method = "mvn"}, \code{"score_bootstrap"}, or \code{"fwb"} predict every
profile under each compared value within each coefficient draw or
weighted refit, and compute the comparisons within that draw before
forming the intervals. For the stored profiles, score-bootstrap and FWB
draws also carry each patient's weight in that draw (\code{score_weight} or
\code{fwb_weight}); averaging a draw's comparisons with these weights
reproduces the \code{\link[=avg_comparisons]{avg_comparisons()}} draw. See \code{\link[=get_draws]{get_draws()}}.
}
The ordinary resampling bootstrap (\code{method = "bootstrap"}) is not
available, because a resampled data set can lack outcome states that the
fixed profiles need.

For \code{rmsb::blrm()} models, each posterior draw gives a comparison for each
profile. The estimate and interval summarize those paired draws, so they
can differ from a comparison of \code{\link[=sops]{sops()}} posterior summaries, especially
with \code{posterior_summary = "median"}.

The result has one row per profile, visit (for SOPs), state set, and
comparison level, plus a column for each profile variable. Requesting fewer
\code{times}, fewer state sets, or fewer profiles reduces its size. Interval
calculations keep every draw of every row, so \code{\link[=inferences]{inferences()}} stops before
drawing when \code{n_draws} times the number of rows exceeds the option
\code{mostr.max_sops_draw_cells} (50 million by default, about 400 MB).
}
